# Line endings, decided by the repo rather than by each clone's core.autocrlf.
#
# This repo pins sha256 digests. `etl/fetch.py` verifies the dataset card against
# f49d9e28..., and the contract checks read their expectations out of that same
# file. Git's default text handling rewrites CRLF to LF in the blob, which changes
# the bytes and makes a pinned checksum fail on a fresh Linux checkout - so the
# card is marked -text and stored exactly as published.
docs/upstream/dataset_card.md  -text

# The source CSVs and the generated fixture are never committed (see .gitignore),
# but if a reviewer points the fetcher at a local copy the same rule applies:
# checksums describe bytes, and bytes do not have line endings to normalise.
data/**                -text
tests/fixtures/**      -text

# Everything else normalises to LF everywhere, so a Windows working copy and a CI
# runner see identical files and `git diff` stays quiet about whitespace.
*.py     text eol=lf
*.sql    text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.toml   text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.txt    text eol=lf
*.cfg    text eol=lf
.gitignore   text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# Never let a diff renderer try to inline these.
*.duckdb binary
*.png    binary
*.pdf    binary
